ID: UC-02
Vision-Based Remote Troubleshooting
Actor: Non-Technical User (Parent/Client) or Remote Admin
Description: ALIEN utilises vision-based perception to navigate a Graphical User Interface (GUI) autonomously, performing troubleshooting tasks or file organization without requiring the user to understand the OS hierarchy.

2. Flow of Events

  2.1 Main Success Scenario (Happy Path)
    1.	Trigger: Actor issues a natural language request: "Help my mom clear her stuck print queue" or "Locate the 2024 tax PDF and put it on the desktop."
    2.	Visual Perception: ALIEN captures a screen state of the target machine and identifies UI elements (icons, search bars, buttons) using its vision model.
    3.	Navigation: System "clicks" the Start menu, searches for "Printers & Scanners," and navigates to the active queue.
    4.	Action: ALIEN identifies the "Cancel All Documents" button and executes the click.
    5.	Verification: System waits for the UI to update, confirming the queue is empty via visual feedback.
    6.	Confirmation: ALIEN provides a simple status update: "All set! The print queue is cleared and I've moved the tax document to the center of the desktop."

  2.2 Alternative Flows
    A1: Item Not Found
  	If the "2024 Tax" document isn't visible, ALIEN opens the file explorer and uses a system-wide search, reporting progress verbally: "I'm searching the whole computer for that file now..."
  
  2.3 Exception Flows
    E1: UAC/Permission Pop-up
    If a Windows User Account Control (UAC) prompt appears, ALIEN identifies the visual block and asks the local user: "I need you to click 'Yes' on the permission box on your screen so I can continue."
    E2: UI Change/Unexpected App
    If an unexpected pop-up (like an update notification) covers the screen, ALIEN recognizes the obstruction and closes it before resuming the task.

3. Requirements

  Type	                                          Description
Preconditions	ALIEN Desktop Agent installed;   Screen recording/Control permissions granted.
Postconditions	UI task completed;             User is returned to a clean desktop state.
Priority	                                     High (Key for "Mass Market" adoption)


4. Technical Logic
•	Vision-Language Model (VLM): Processes raw pixels into semantic coordinates (e.g., "The 'X' button is at 800, 20").
•	Autonomous GUI Interaction: Mimics human mouse and keyboard events based on visual reasoning rather than just backend API calls.
•	Low-Latency Streaming: Ensures the AI "sees" changes in real-time to avoid clicking on outdated UI states.
